<title>USA: AFTER THE BELL - Quiet prevails after &quot;noisy day&quot;.</title>
<headline>AFTER THE BELL - Quiet prevails after &quot;noisy day&quot;.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-04-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Few stocks were active in after-hours trading Friday, and at least one dealer found relief in the relative quiet.</p>
<p>&quot;It was a noisy day,&quot; he said grimly.</p>
<p>During regular trading hours, the Dow Jones industrial average fell 148 points, and the Nasdaq skidded 28.87 points.</p>
<p>One of the few stocks that bucked the trend on Friday was International Business Machines Corp, which gained 1/2 to 133-3/8. But even IBM was inactive after the bell, traders said.  </p>
<p>The New York Stock Exchange said session one volume was 10,700, compared to 900 on Thursday. Session two volume was 1,865,000, while on Thursday it was 4,547,300.</p>
